actor levels, while thyroid hormone markedly boosts plasminogen activator inhibitor-1 (PAI-1) by up to 100% in hypothyroid patients undergoing replacement therapy [Data: Relationships (3008, 3009, 3362)]. These findings suggest that thyroid status critically influences thrombotic risk, and clinicians should consider coagulation monitoring during thyroid hormone treatment, especially in patients with preexisting cardiovascular conditions.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors carry a risk of hyperkalemia\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The drug class 碳酸酐酶抑制剂 (carbonic anhydrase inhibitors) is associated with hyperkalemia (高钾现象), particularly when used in combination with other diuretics or in specific clinical contexts [Data: Relationships (14835)]. This adverse reaction is notable because many diuretics cause hypokalemia, making hyperkalemia from this class a counterintuitive and potentially dangerous effect if not anticipated. This interaction underscores the complexity of electrolyte management in patients receiving multiple renal-affecting medications.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Congenital adrenal disorders are core indications for corticosteroid replacement\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Both 醋酸氢化可的松 and 醋酸可的松 are formally indicated for congenital adrenal hyperplasia (including both '先天性肾上腺皮质增生症' and '先天性肾上腺增生症'), reflecting their essential role in enzyme-deficient glucocorticoid synthesis pathways [Data: Entities (289, 288); Relationships (24999, 24956)]. These conditions require lifelong hormone replacement to prevent adrenal crisis and support normal development, positioning these drugs as life-sustaining therapeutics rather than merely symptomatic treatments. Their use in these genetic disorders highlights the intersection of pharmacology and rare disease management.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Autoimmune and inflammatory diseases are secondary but importan